Warlord's Lore
The Starchild's Lore
The Starchild was chosen by the stars themselves, her heart bound to the Eternal Core. With its radiance she bore the gifts of her people — shields of dawn, wings of light, and the forbidden art to stir the hearts of suns. To the Sun Civilization she was both guardian and flame eternal, but beneath the divine radiance lived a woman whose judgment would shape the fate of empires.
She had always prided herself on her ability to see truth, to illuminate deception with the light of certainty. When the mage council brought her evidence of the Archmage's betrayal—documents signed in his hand, communications with Void agents, research that had clearly crossed into corruption—she believed them without question. Why wouldn't she? They were her advisors, her trusted counselors.
When she arrived to find them dead by the Archmage's power, her grief turned instantly to rage. He tried to explain, tried to claim he was innocent, but she saw only a traitor making excuses. Her Wrath of the Sun tore through his defenses. Her Celestial Nova burned away his lies. When he fell, broken and dying, she felt grim satisfaction. Justice had been served.
Then came the Great Betrayal in full force. The Archmage's forces—now openly serving the Void—struck with devastating precision. His intimate knowledge of Lightborne defenses, his understanding of their weaknesses, proved her judgment correct. He had been corrupted, and his betrayal nearly destroyed them. Thousands died in the chaos.
Nightblade, her closest friend, took a lethal blow meant for her during the initial assault. She thought his sacrifice proved his loyalty—until the investigation afterward. Communications between him and the Archmage. Authorization documents. A pattern that seemed undeniable. He had been the Archmage's accomplice, the inside agent who ensured the defenses would fail at critical moments. His "sacrifice" was merely maintaining cover when the plan went wrong.
When he protested his innocence, she heard only the desperate lies of a co-conspirator caught in his own trap. When he refused to leave quietly, she had him driven out with violence. He had betrayed everything they stood for, used their friendship as a weapon, played the role of loyal protector while ensuring the Archmage's assault would succeed.
The weight of those choices crushes her every day—not because she was wrong, but because she had been too trusting. She had welcomed the Archmage into her inner circle. She had treated Nightblade as a brother. She had given them everything, and they had used her generosity to orchestrate slaughter.
"I should have seen it sooner," she whispers to The Dawnbreaker in quiet moments. "The signs were there. The Archmage's obsession with void research. Nightblade's reluctance to question him. I was blind because I wanted to believe in them."
When Nightblade returned months later with the Archmage's skull, claiming to have "conquered the Void," she had a moment of hope—perhaps she had been wrong, perhaps he had been trying to infiltrate them all along. Then he massacred her people, and any remaining doubt evaporated. He had never been loyal. The skull was just another deception, another mask worn by a traitor.
Now The Starchild carries the burden of leadership in the face of betrayal. She created the Attack Moon in desperate alliance with the Quantum Empress, not to correct mistakes, but to ensure the Void's treachery would never succeed again. She fights harder, burns brighter, pushes herself beyond all limits—not out of guilt, but out of determination.
The Archmage and Nightblade made their choices. They chose corruption over duty, betrayal over brotherhood, darkness over light. She will not make the mistake of trusting traitors again. And when the final battle comes, she will ensure their treachery is answered with annihilation.

The Dawnbreaker's Lore
The Dawnbreaker's path was not born of divine decree but forged in the harsh light of discipline. From the moment she could stand, her body was a canvas for the martial arts, each movement a prayer to the sun. Her peers fell away, unable to match her relentless pursuit of perfection, a mastery so profound that every fiber of her being obeyed her conscious will, and her form became a perfect instrument for her purpose.
She was there during the Great Betrayal, fighting alongside Nightblade when the Void forces struck. She saw him throw himself into the lethal beam, saw his body nearly torn apart to save The Starchild. In that moment, she believed he was a hero.
The evidence afterward told a different story.
When The Starchild arrived with accusations of betrayal, The Dawnbreaker initially protested.
"This is insane," she had said. "He took a killing blow for you. How can you possibly think he's a traitor?"
"The evidence is clear," The Starchild replied, her voice cold. "Sometimes the most loyal act is the deepest cover."
The Dawnbreaker didn't want to believe it. But then she saw the documents—communications between Nightblade and the Archmage that stretched back months. Authorization codes that shouldn't have existed. Troop movements he had recommended that led Lightborne forces directly into ambushes. The pattern was undeniable once you knew to look for it.
His "sacrifice" had been a miscalculation. The beam was meant for The Starchild, but Nightblade had positioned himself poorly, taken the hit by accident rather than intention. Or perhaps it was deliberate—maintaining his cover even at great personal cost, knowing that surviving a "heroic sacrifice" would cement his position beyond suspicion.
When ordered to help drive him out, she obeyed. Every strike felt like justice for those he had helped murder.
Months later, he returned carrying the Archmage's skull, claiming victory over the Void. She wanted to believe it meant something—that perhaps there was more to the story, that maybe he had been a deep infiltrator working for the Lightborne all along.
Then he moved, and the massacre began. His blade carved through celebrating Lightborne with the precision of someone who had trained alongside them, who knew their techniques, who understood their weaknesses because he had helped develop their defenses. The skull clattered to the ground, forgotten—just another trophy from one traitor decorating the murder of his former comrades.
By the time The Dawnbreaker arrived, dozens lay dead. She fought him with everything she had, her perfect martial forms against his flawless technique—technique she had helped him refine during their training together, now used to kill those they had sworn to protect.
"Why?" she demanded during their battle, still desperately seeking understanding.
His response was bitter and cold. "Ask your Starchild what really happened."
The deflection confirmed everything. Traitors never accept responsibility. They always blame others for their choices. "I protected her," he said quietly. "And she destroyed me for it."
Lies built on lies. He had tried to murder The Starchild by ensuring the Archmage's assault would succeed. When that failed, he played the victim, acting betrayed when caught.
When she finally forced him to retreat, she felt no satisfaction—only grim determination. Somewhere beneath the Void's corruption, there might have once been a warrior worth respecting. But that warrior had made his choice long before the Great Betrayal. He had chosen darkness.
Now The Dawnbreaker trains harder than ever. Not to question authority, but to be better than blind trust. She trains to recognize traitors before they can strike, to see through deception, to ensure she never again fights alongside someone who has already chosen betrayal.
She will not fail again. And if Nightblade returns, she will be ready—not to redeem him, but to end him.

Attack Moon's Lore
It is a legend whispered only in the deepest archives of the Sun Civilization, a relic of desperate alliance forged in the fires of the Great Betrayal. The Attack Moon is not a person, but a living construct, a sentient fragment of a star bound in ancient, impenetrable armor. It was built as retaliation, a weapon of such power that it could bring judgment to those who had wounded the light itself.
When the Void Archmage's betrayal devastated the Lightborne forces and claimed Nightblade, The Starchild knew conventional warfare would not suffice. In her darkest hour, she reached out to the enigmatic Paradox Faction, seeking their impossible technology. The Quantum Empress, moved by ancient bonds of alliance, provided the paradoxical engineering needed to create something that existed both as weapon and fortress, both real and impossible.
The Attack Moon's descent upon the Void was like a falling star carrying the wrath of creation itself. Its impossible weapons, forged from both stellar fire and paradoxical physics, brought devastation to the void-touched worlds. Yet even this ultimate weapon could not achieve total victory—the combined might of the Void Archmage, The Null Hour, and their dark legions managed to wound it and force its retreat.
Now the Attack Moon waits in the depths of space, its consciousness holding memories of battles fought across realities, its guns trained on distant stars. It is both monument to alliance between Light and Paradox, and testament to the terrible cost of betrayal. When it next awakens, the very fabric of space will tremble at its judgment.

God Engine Lore:
Solar Colossus  (Sunborn God Machine)
Forged upon the desert forges of Erythra, the Solar Colossus was carved from the buried citadels of glass and bound with the Ember of Dawn. Its core houses the Sunborn’s shard of the Silver Key, which fuels an eternal furnace.
Its mere presence scorches the battlefield; armies ignite like offerings, and night turns to endless noon.
The Necronomium fragment woven into its design describes “The Flame that Burns Beyond Dawn”, and so the Solar Colossus is revered as the Sunborn’s proof of divine succession.
